1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 d6 4. Bb5 Bd7 5. c3 Nf6 6. Qc2 Ne7 7. Bxd7+ Nxd7 8.
O-O Ng6 9. Qb3 b6 10. Ng5 Qf6 11. f4 exf4 12. Nh3 Be7 13. Bxf4 O-O 14. Nd2 Rae8
15. Bg5 Qe6 16. Qd1 c5 17. d5 Qe5 18. Rf5 Bxg5 19. Rxe5 ({"This is a very
interesting position. Probably nine players out of ten would have followed the
same course as the Oxonian, and taken the queen for two pieces. Curiously
enough, it would have been better to be satisfied with a slighter gain, and
continue as follows:} 19. Nxg5 Qe7 20. Qh5 h6 21. Nxf7 Rxf7 22. Qxg6 Rxf5 23.
Qxf5 Rf8 24. Qh3 {, with a pawn ahead." (The Field)}) 19... Be3+ 20. Kh1 Ndxe5
21. Nf3 Ng4 22. Qa4 Nf6 ({"An incomprehensible retreat. He should have played} 
22... Re7 {, defending his a-pawn, and then double his rooks and play a
waiting game. It was close upon six o'clock, the time fixed for adjudication,
and in this position the umpire would have probably given it as a draw." (The
Field) (n.b. Stockfish 14 prefers the move played, judging that the mistake
came on the subsequent move - JS)}) 23. Qxa7 Rxe4 ({Stockfish 14 prefers} 23...
c4 $16) 24. Qxb6 Nf4 25. Nxf4 Bxf4 26. Qb3 Rfe8 27. g3 Re3 28. Rf1 Bh6 29. a4
Re2 30. a5 Ne4 31. Qb5 Nf6 {Adjudicated a win for White by L Hoffer. Sources:
Evening News and Post (London), 29 March 1890; 'The Chess-Monthly', April 1890;
Worthing Gazette, 2 April 1890; The Field, 5 April 1890 (Contributed by Brian
Denman; list of sources updated, 10 May 2021) - n.b. I have not included all
the detailed annotations published in The Field, just the most significant
ones - JS} 1-0

