{Opening: 1 e4 e5 2 Nf3 Nc6 3 Bb5 Nd4... "Board No.6.—F. E. Jelly (Oxford) played also a Ruy Lopez, which R. C. Stephenson (Cambridge) defended with 3...Nd4, an inferior defence (favoured by Bird), which gave White the usual advantage, which he ought to obtain against this defence; but he exchanged queens early, and allowed the Cantab to institute a telling counter attack. The following is the position at the most interesting stage of the game, after White's 26.Bd1."} 26... hxg3 ({Better would have been} 26... Nxf2+ {followed by 27...hxg3, winning the exchange (Hoffer)}) 27. fxg3 Ne3 28. f6 Rxg3 29. Re1 Ng2 30. Rg4 Rxg4 (30... Rd3 $19 {Hoffer}) 31. Bxg4+ Kd8 ({Stockfish 17:} 31... Kb8 $17) 32. Re7 Rh6 ({"Any other rook's move would have lost." This erroneous assessment appears in both The Field and The Chess Monthly, which then go on to give the following line:} 32... Rh4 33. Bf5 Rh5 34. Bc2 Nf4 ({Stockfish 17:} 34... Rh6 35. Rxf7 Ne3 36. Bd3 Ng4 $17) 35. Rxf7 Rg5 ({Stockfish 17:} 35... Rh6 $15) 36. Rxb7 ({Stockfish 17:} 36. Rf8+ Kd7 37. f7 $1 $18) 36... Ke8 37. Ba4 Nd3 38. Bxc6+ Kf8 39. Bxd5 {"and wins" but Black can now simply take the bishop with the rook and Black wins. JS}) (32... Rg8 {is also playable. JS}) 33. Rxf7 Ke8 34. Be6 Nf4 35. Re7+ Kf8 {Draw by perpetual check. Sources: The Field, 5 April 1890, p503; The Chess Monthly, Vol.11 (1890), p239-240} 1/2-1/2

