{Board No.7.â€”E. B. James (Cambridge), having the move, opened with 1.d4, and
L. C. Crump (Oxford) replied with 1...d5. Considering that this was the last
board in order of merit, the game was above the average of what would have
been expected. Both players were soon after the few opening moves thrown upon
their own resources, and struggled on manfully with quite an irregular and
difficult position. In the ending the Cantab managed to win a piece, but at
the expense of three pawns, and could only manage to draw, the Oxonian not
being anxious at all about the fate of his game, but rather disappointed that
his opponent would not venture to try for a win. The following is the position
after Black's 38th move:} 39. Bf1 Ke5 ({Stockfish 14} 39... Kc5 $1 $19) 40. Be2
Kd5 41. Bd1 Ke5 42. Be2 Kd5 {Agreed drawn. Source: The Field, 5 April 1890, p503
} (42... Kd5 {"If} 43. Kf4 b4 44. cxb4 Kd4 {and the two passed pawns win
against the bishop."}) 1/2-1/2

